---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Architecture from "../components/diagrams/Architecture.astro";
import Flow from "../components/diagrams/Flow.astro";
import { href } from "../lib";

// Links point at exact commits, so they always show the files as described.
const pw = "https://github.com/leat-consulting/platform-workflows/blob/0328c4b9e797d6a0fe1adb857bb02356c7b6000f";
const site = "https://github.com/leat-consulting/leat-site/blob/b77884633dcac121635d729bbea4c13c9343054e";
import { breadcrumbs } from "../schema";
---

<Base
  title="How we build"
  description="How Leat Consulting's own delivery platform works: one reviewed gate for every change, automatic checks, signed builds, and the principles behind it."
  schema={[breadcrumbs([{ name: "How we build", path: "how-we-build/" }])]}
>
  <section class="band band--hero">
    <span class="eyebrow">How we build</span>
    <h1>One reviewed gate for every change</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      The systems that build and deploy software are now a favourite target, because they hold the keys to
      production. This is how we protect our own, using the same setup we put in place for clients. All of
      it is public.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="gate-heading">
    <span class="eyebrow">The architecture</span>
    <h2 id="gate-heading">However a change starts, it reaches production one way</h2>
    <p>
      Whether a change is written by a person, by an AI tool or by an automatic update, it reaches production
      only through a pull request that passes its checks and is approved by a person.
    </p>
    <Architecture />
  </section>

  <section class="band band--tint" aria-labelledby="flow-heading">
    <span class="eyebrow">The flow</span>
    <h2 id="flow-heading">From pull request to live, with one human step</h2>
    <p>
      The only human step is the approval. Everything before it is checked automatically, and everything
      after it happens by itself, so what was reviewed is exactly what goes live.
    </p>
    <Flow />
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="principles-heading">
    <span class="eyebrow">Principles</span>
    <h2 id="principles-heading">Five rules we hold to</h2>
    <ol class="principles">
      <li><strong>Least privilege by default</strong> <span>Every automated job starts with read-only access and is given only what it needs.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Know what you run</strong> <span>Third-party pipeline components are fixed to exact versions, allowed by name, and checked on every change.</span></li>
      <li><strong>No long-lived secrets</strong> <span>Pipelines use short-lived credentials issued per run, so there is nothing lasting to steal.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Automation proposes, people approve</strong> <span>AI tools and update bots open pull requests; they can never approve or merge them.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Rules as code</strong> <span>Protection rules are written down, reviewed like code, and checked regularly so nobody can quietly weaken them.</span></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="band band--tint" aria-labelledby="tiers-heading">
    <span class="eyebrow">What you need, and when</span>
    <h2 id="tiers-heading">Three tiers, not everything at once</h2>
    <p>Not every team needs everything here. Start with the first tier and add the rest as you need it.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Tier</th><th scope="col">What it covers</th><th scope="col">You need it when</th><th scope="col">In our repositories</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Start here</th>
            <td>Pull requests and approval on the main branch, secret scanning that blocks leaked keys, automatic dependency updates, read-only pipeline permissions, checks on every pull request.</td>
            <td>Always. They are cheap, and most teams are missing some of them.</td>
            <td><a href={`${pw}/governance/rulesets/default-branch.json`}>branch rules</a>, <a href={`${site}/.github/workflows/pr.yml`}>pull request checks</a>, <a href={`${site}/.github/dependabot.yml`}>update settings</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Add next</th>
            <td>Pipeline components fixed to exact versions and allowed by name, security scanning in CI, a separate identity for AI tools, short-lived cloud access.</td>
            <td>When you depend on third-party pipeline components, use AI coding tools, or deploy to the cloud from CI.</td>
            <td><a href={`${pw}/governance/actions-policy.json`}>allowed components</a>, <a href={`${pw}/.github/workflows/security-scan.yml`}>security scan</a>, <a href={`${pw}/bin/README.md`}>AI identity</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">For regulated teams</th>
            <td>Signed and verified builds, releases that can never be altered, protection rules checked for drift.</td>
            <td>When auditors, regulators or enterprise customers ask you to prove how your software was built.</td>
            <td><a href={`${pw}/.github/workflows/pages-deploy.yml`}>signed deploys</a>, <a href={`${pw}/.github/workflows/container-build.yml`}>signed images</a>, <a href={`${pw}/governance/README.md`}>drift checks</a></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Our own setup runs on GitHub, which most of our clients use. For teams on GitLab we build the same
      patterns with GitLab's equivalents: CI/CD components, protected branches with merge request approvals,
      and OIDC from GitLab to AWS.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="next-heading">
    <h2 id="next-heading">Want this for your team?</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href={href("platform-setup/")}>Delivery platform setup</a> puts it in place in your own repositories,
      or start with a <a href={href("platform-review/")}>Platform review</a> to see where you stand.
    </p>
    <div class="actions"><a class="button" href={href("contact/")}>Talk to us</a></div>
  </section>
</Base>
